+# The Daily Grind origin migration
+
+This static app builds separate deployments for the old and new origins. No backend or development server is required. The source checkout is intentionally unconfigured; no customer data is sent to placeholder domains.
+
+```sh
+pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
+OLD_ORIGIN=https://old.your-domain.com NEW_ORIGIN=https://app.your-domain.com pnpm build
+pnpm test
+```
+
+Deploy `dist/new` to NEW_ORIGIN first, then `dist/old` to OLD_ORIGIN, both at `/` over HTTPS. Both origins must share a registrable domain; the build validates against the Public Suffix List, including private suffixes. The build fails without explicit origins. Do not deploy the example domains.
+
+Set `OLD_APP_ID` to the existing app's **exact resolved manifest ID** (default `/`, resolved against OLD_ORIGIN). An installation without an explicit ID uses its original start URL as its identity; supply that URL if different. Keep the old app's name, icons, start URL and scope consistent with its original deployed manifest when adapting this starter. This repository originally had no manifest or persistence layer, so there is no existing installed identity to discover here.
+
+The new manifest declares `migrate_from`; the old manifest signals `migrate_to`. The old origin serves `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` authorizing the destination app ID. Serve it as `application/json` without authentication or redirects; serve the manifest as `application/manifest+json`. Configure your static host to expose `.well-known` files. Do not redirect the old root, manifest or transfer assets until settings have been moved. If the host sends Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy, allow the same-site transfer popup to retain its opener (do not use `same-origin` on these pages); the file backup works when opener isolation is unavoidable.
+
+## Configuration preservation
+
+Browser migration moves the installation and its OS shortcuts, subject to browser support and user acceptance of the update. It does **not** copy localStorage, IndexedDB, cookies or permissions. Settings are copied separately through a user-initiated popup with exact origin, window and nonce checks. No settings are placed in URLs. The source data is retained; failed storage writes attempt rollback and never report success. The destination acknowledges only after saving.
+
+`CONFIG_KEYS` is a JSON array of localStorage keys that contain user configuration, defaulting to `["daily-grind:preferences"]`. Set it to the real application's preference keys in the build environment. This starter did not previously save configuration. Only listed keys are copied; authentication tokens and unrelated data must not be listed. Matching destination keys are replaced, absent source keys leave destination preferences alone, and payloads are limited to 64 KiB. For example:
+
+```sh
+OLD_ORIGIN=https://old.your-domain.com NEW_ORIGIN=https://app.your-domain.com \
+CONFIG_KEYS='["theme","coffee-preferences"]' OLD_APP_ID=/ \
+pnpm build
+```
+
+Any future IndexedDB configuration requires an explicit adapter; authenticated data should use account-based server synchronization. Users sign in again and grant origin-scoped permissions on the new site as needed. Transfer settings **before** accepting the installation update and keep the old origin available for late transfers.
+
+For browsers without automatic installation migration, the old site provides a destination link and manual installation instructions. Settings transfer still works using the popup or downloaded JSON backup, imported at `/migration.html` on the new site. Manual reinstall cannot guarantee preservation of existing OS shortcuts. No unsupported migration API is called or falsely feature-detected.
+
+## Validation
+
+`pnpm test` covers deployment origin checks, spoofed message origins, configuration allowlisting, malformed/oversized backups and storage failure rollback. Build output can be inspected as static files. Browser-managed installation and OS shortcut updates require deployment and a supporting browser; automated unit tests do not establish that a live installation has migrated.
+
+The manifest format follows [Chrome's origin migration documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/seamless-pwa-origin-migration), including the object-shaped `migrate_to` signal. Chrome documents the feature starting with version 150; other browsers may need the manual fallback.
